From 80a9bd7a1dbbe3b62e1cb8ae480ba3f1b65b4220 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Dawid=20Wr=C3=B3bel?= <me@dawidwrobel.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 11:03:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 24/41] tee: qseecom: allow only one supplicant at a time

There is one request slot, so a second process on the privileged device races
the first on TEE_IOC_SUPPL_RECV and the two consume each other's requests --
and since a request is answered by whoever calls SUPPL_SEND next, the wrong
reply reaches the wrong application. The documentation warned about this while
the driver permitted it.

The listener a request belongs to is reported in arg.func precisely so that one
process can serve them all, so refusing the second costs nothing.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
---
 drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c | 19 ++++++++++++++++---
 1 file changed, 16 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
index b1b586612..d38307e27 100644
--- a/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
+++ b/drivers/tee/qseecom/core.c
@@ -1173,9 +1173,22 @@ static int qseecom_tee_supp_open(struct tee_context *ctx)
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
 
-	mutex_lock(&qtee->supp.mutex);
-	qtee->supp.users++;
-	mutex_unlock(&qtee->supp.mutex);
+	/*
+	 * One supplicant, because there is one request slot. A second process
+	 * on this device would race the first on TEE_IOC_SUPPL_RECV and the
+	 * two would consume each other's requests -- and since a request is
+	 * answered by whoever calls SUPPL_SEND next, the wrong reply would go
+	 * to the wrong application. The listener a request belongs to is in
+	 * arg.func precisely so that one process can serve them all.
+	 */
+	scoped_guard(mutex, &qtee->supp.mutex) {
+		if (qtee->supp.users) {
+			qseecom_tee_release(ctx);
+			return -EBUSY;
+		}
+
+		qtee->supp.users++;
+	}
 
 	return 0;
 }
